<article class="env-remark" id="B333D3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/B333D3">Remark <span data-tag="B333D3">5.5.2.6</span>.</a> Now that we know that every presentable $\infty $-category $\calC $ has arbitrary limits, we can apply an argument dual to that of Remark <a href="/tag/ADCB5F" data-tag="ADCB5F">5.5.1.7</a> to show that $\calC $ is <em>cotensored over $\SSet $</em>. In other words, for any $C \in \calC $ and every simplicial set $X$, there exists an object $C^ X \in \calC $ (well defined up to equivalence) together with a collection of natural isomorphisms </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[ \bHom _{\calC }(C',C^ X) \simeq \bHom _{\calC }(C',C)^{X} \]
</div>
  <p> in the homotopy category $\calH $. </p>
</article>